A||||7||2birds||clutch troubleshooting||||3||
Z||000000||2birds||07-25-2000||12:11 AM||||Before I tear into a clutch replacement after only 35,000 miles, I'm curious to see if anybody has experienced a similar symptom.<BR>Basically, my clutch only slips after a 20-30 mile interstate (70 mph) drive.  I've tried to recreate it during the drive back and forth to work where I routinely see a 55 mph stretch for about 5 miles, but I can't make it come back in any gear.  After driving on the Interstate, it'll slip at 40mph in 5th gear reallly easily.  <BR>I have a posssible theory, based in my supposedly adequate knowledge of fluids and thermodynamics. Engine temps read the same, but could exhaust heat be causing the (non-cmpressible) fluid in the clutch hydraulics to heat and expand enough to move the slave cylinder piston, partially actuating the pressure plate diapragm?  Is ther supposed to be any free play between the slave plunger and the throwout bearing arm actuator?  <BR>I used to have trouble with the clutch not diengaging fully after long highway drives, but bleeding the hydraulics eventually made that go away. Now, if I have all of the air out, the fluid has to go somewhere as it heats up and its volume increases.  That's my theory; any comments are greatly appreciated, especially from those who really know what's going on (those who don't are appreciated, too)<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000001||Patrick||07-25-2000||01:00 AM||||<BR>I don't really know what's going on, but... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>The clutch hydraulic system in the Fiero, being totally self-adjusting, should be able to compensate for any temperature differences of the fluid. There doesn't need to be any "free play". Is there any chance that the piston in your slave is bottomed out (inside the cylinder) when the clutch is engaged? This could be the result of an improper (too long) pushrod, or the slave being mounted too close (wrong side of bracket) to the clutch release lever. (Or a combination of all of the above.) One way to check this is to use the <I>Archie</I> method of bleeding the slave. Open the bleeder on the slave, and with vice-grips locked onto the pushrod, you should be able to pull the pushrod towards the bleeder valve. If it doesn't want to come any in that direction, I'd say you have something wonky that could cause the problems you're experiencing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mattsfiero||07-25-2000||03:30 AM||||Well I can tel you I have enough trouble with my clutch as well. It tends to slowly loose pressure either engaging in a gear or locking me out of gear after holding the clutch in. Some of the things I have head from those here in the forum is to check the Diameter of the slave cyclinder. If you have replaced the slave cylinder you may have put the 13/16" diameter on and may need the 15/16" instead. this may be the only problem (if you have a slave that is too small the pressure may activate the clutch causing it to slip out of gear while running. Try this and let me know how it turns out.<P>Matt  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||07-25-2000||03:29 PM||||<BR>Matt, no offence, but I don't think that the slave <I>diameter</I> would have ANY effect on whether a clutch could fully <I>engage</I> or not. A smaller diameter slave used in place of a larger one would allow the travel to be greater when the clutch is <I>dis</I>engaged (which is why I suggested in the other thread that you make sure you have the 13/16 inch one on your Fiero), but I sincerely doubt that it would have any bearing on the problem 2birds has.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||07-25-2000||06:44 PM||||My slave cylinder has never been worked on, but I rebuilt the clutch master cylinder two years ago, since it was leaking into the passenger compartment.  <BR>I didn't think there was any way to put the slave cylinder on wrong.  It's been four years since I replaced the clutch, and the take up point has always been near the middle of the pedal travel. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mattsfiero||07-25-2000||06:56 PM||||Patrick.. I was only offering information to 2bird that I have learned. The only reason why I suggested that is if he is using the smaller one there might be a possibility that it is not allowing the room for expansion like it should.<P>Matt  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||07-25-2000||07:07 PM||||Have you explored the obvious fluid leakage from the engine rear main seal OR the transmission input shaft seal?<P>Here's an abstract one. Could it be possible that the rubber line from the clutch-slave to the steel line has swelled or is decaying inside, and is acting as check-valve as the engine heats up? A long shot, but it sounds as if you are in desperate measures! Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||07-27-2000||10:42 PM||||Thanks for the input, as always.<P>The worn out hose idea is interesting.  I wonder what a replacement would cost, if it is even available.<BR>The rear main, cam cover, and input shaft ideas require enough tear down that I'd throw in a new clutch anyway.  My cam cover was leaking slightly until I put this clutch in.  Rear main was OK, but the pan gasket was all jacked up by the dealer that did the clutch the first time. so I fixed it, too.  Haven't noticed any dripping oil since I replaced the distributor O-ring. ||||reg||1
